{{!-- The bar every Surface wears, in the two shapes this theme draws.

Which shape a Surface gets is named at the call site, in the template, because the shape is a
property of the Surface and the Surface is the template — see the route map in DECISIONS.md.
It is not sniffed from the render context: a Surface's context depends on the buyer's
routes.yaml (this install's Feed reports `archive`, a bare install's reports `index`) and an
error page reports no context at all, so context is the one thing that cannot name a Surface.

	{{> "chrome/header"}}                 the site bar: mark, nav, search, Mode, Subscribe
	{{> "chrome/header" auth=true}}       the members bar: mark, a way back, Mode

ONE BAR ON EVERY READING SURFACE, WHICH IS A DIVERGENCE. The design draws two: this one on the
homepage and the Feed, and a "reading bar" — 2px smaller in both the mark and the wordmark, and
with no ⌕ — on the post, the archives, the Pages and the error Surfaces. Read one page at a time
that reads as a missing search control rather than as a quieter chrome, and the publication's only
visible way into search existed on two Surfaces out of twenty-seven. The `compact` parameter is
gone rather than defaulted, so there is no second shape to drift.

Order inside the bar is fixed by the design: mark · nav · search · Mode · Subscribe · menu.

Search hands off to Ghost. The platform ships its own search — index, popup, keyboard — and
sodo-search binds to any element carrying `data-ghost-search`, so the design's ⌕ button is
that element and this theme writes no search code at all. Two measured details from sodo-search
1.8 (`getCustomTriggerButtons`/`addKeyboardShortcuts`) are why the attribute is on a real
control rather than on something hidden: the ⌘K shortcut is registered only if the page carries
at least one trigger, and the below-breakpoint overlay carries one of its own on every Surface.
With the ⌕ now everywhere, the shortcut and the button agree everywhere too.

The Mode switch renders once, here, on every shape. The reference build learned that the
hard way: a floating toggle placed per page turned into 22 copies. --}}
<header
	class="bg-surface border-border border-b"
	{{#unless auth}}x-data="siteMenu" x-on:resize.window="resized()"{{/unless}}>
	{{!-- The one control the design does not draw and every keyboard visitor needs. It has no
	pixels until it has focus, so it costs the reference nothing. --}}
	<a
		href="#main"
		class="btn btn-primary font-ui sr-only rounded-sm px-4 py-2 text-sm font-semibold no-underline focus:not-sr-only focus:absolute focus:top-2 focus:left-2 focus:z-50">{{t "Skip to content"}}</a>

	<div
		{{#unless auth}}x-ref="bar"{{/unless}}
		class="container flex flex-wrap items-center gap-y-4 {{#if auth}}gap-x-6 py-[18px]{{else}}gap-x-[30px] py-[22px]{{/if}}">

		{{#if auth}}
			{{> "chrome/wordmark" wrapper="mr-auto" logoHeight="h-4" titleSize="text-[22px]"}}

			<a
				href="{{@site.url}}"
				class="font-ui text-muted hover:text-accent text-sm no-underline"><span aria-hidden="true">←</span> {{t "Back to site"}}</a>

			{{> "chrome/mode-toggle"}}
		{{else}}
			{{!-- Below the nav breakpoint the nav is gone and the mark takes over pushing the
			controls to the right edge. A publication that has emptied its primary menu is in that
			state at EVERY width, so this branch drops the landmark (ADR 0003) and hands `mr-auto`
			to the mark outright. Two calls rather than one with a computed wrapper, because
			Handlebars takes no block helper inside a hash argument. --}}
			{{#if @site.navigation}}
				{{> "chrome/wordmark" wrapper="max-nav:mr-auto" logoHeight="h-[19px]" titleSize="text-[26px]"}}

				<nav
					aria-label="{{t "Main navigation"}}"
					class="nav:flex mr-auto hidden flex-wrap items-center gap-[22px]">{{navigation variant="bar"}}</nav>
			{{else}}
				{{> "chrome/wordmark" wrapper="mr-auto" logoHeight="h-[19px]" titleSize="text-[26px]"}}
			{{/if}}

			{{!-- An inline SVG rather than the ⌕ glyph: U+2315 draws at roughly half its em
			box, so inside a 36px pill it reads as a speck, and how big a speck depends on
			whichever mono font the visitor's system resolves. 18px of `currentColor` is the
			same mark in both Modes at every font stack. --}}
			<button
				type="button"
				aria-label="{{t "Search"}}"
				data-ghost-search
				class="btn border-border text-text hover:bg-surface-2 rounded-pill max-nav:hidden grid size-9 shrink-0 cursor-pointer place-items-center border bg-transparent">
				<svg
					aria-hidden="true"
					viewBox="0 0 24 24"
					fill="none"
					stroke="currentColor"
					stroke-width="1.9"
					stroke-linecap="round"
					class="block size-[18px]">
					<circle cx="10.5" cy="10.5" r="6.5"></circle>
					<line x1="15.4" y1="15.4" x2="20" y2="20"></line>
				</svg>
			</button>

			{{> "chrome/mode-toggle"}}

			{{> "chrome/cta" class="btn btn-primary font-ui max-nav:hidden rounded-sm px-[18px] py-2.5 text-sm font-semibold no-underline"}}

			<button
				type="button"
				aria-label="{{t "Menu"}}"
				x-ref="burger"
				x-on:click="toggle()"
				x-bind:aria-expanded="menuOpen"
				aria-expanded="false"
				aria-controls="site-menu"
				class="btn border-border text-text hover:bg-surface-2 rounded-pill nav:hidden grid size-9 flex-none cursor-pointer place-items-center border bg-transparent">
				<span
					aria-hidden="true"
					class="flex size-9 flex-col items-center justify-center gap-[5px]">
					<span
						class="bg-text block h-[1.5px] w-4 transition-transform"
						x-bind:class="menuOpen ? 'translate-y-[3.25px] rotate-45' : ''"></span>
					<span
						class="bg-text block h-[1.5px] w-4 transition-transform"
						x-bind:class="menuOpen ? '-translate-y-[3.25px] -rotate-45' : ''"></span>
				</span>
			</button>
		{{/if}}
	</div>

	{{#unless auth}}
		{{> "chrome/menu"}}
	{{/unless}}
</header>
